{#
  What has been said about a post: likes, boosts and mentions as facepiles
  grouped by kind, and the replies as a thread, in the source design's markup
  (decision-16, TASK-84).

  Rendered from `layouts/post.njk` with the `conversation` on the context,
  which is only there when there is something in it, so a post nobody has
  answered renders none of this. A site replaces the whole section with a
  `partials/conversation.njk` of its own in the theme it wears; the shape it is
  handed is documented in the theme README under "The conversation".

  The macros come first because Nunjucks defines one where it stands rather
  than hoisting it, and the two sections below call them.
#}
{#
  One face in a facepile: somebody's avatar, linked to wherever they are.

  Without an avatar there is nothing to recognise them by, so the emoji badge
  of their kind stands in and their name is printed beside it — which is what
  a mention from a site with no `h-card` photo, and a like from an actor
  nobody here follows, look like.
#}
{% macro face(item, icon, href) %}
{%- set name = item.author.name -%}
{%- if href %}<a class="u-url" href="{{ href }}" title="{{ name }}" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">{% else %}<span title="{{ name }}">{% endif %}
{%- if item.author.avatar -%}
<img class="avatar u-photo" src="{{ item.author.avatar }}" alt="{{ name }}" width="32" height="32" loading="lazy">
{%- else -%}
<span class="reaction-icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ icon }}</span><span class="reaction-name">{{ name }}</span>
{%- endif -%}
{%- if href %}</a>{% else %}</span>{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}

{#
  One kind of reaction: a title carrying the label and how many there are, and
  the people behind them as a facepile. Nothing at all for a kind nothing has.

  `source` says where a face links. A like or a boost is a person, so it links
  to them; a mention is a page that wrote about this one, and the page is the
  thing worth reading, so it links there and falls back to its author.
#}
{% macro group(items, label, kind, icon, source=false) %}
{% if items.length %}
<div class="reaction-group {{ kind }}">
  <h2 class="reaction-title">{{ label }} ({{ items.length }})</h2>
  <div class="facepile">
    {%- for item in items %}{{ face(item, icon, (item.url or item.author.url) if source else (item.author.url or item.url)) }}{% endfor %}
  </div>
</div>
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{#
  One reply and everything under it. A macro rather than an include so it can
  call itself: a reply that answers a reply is the same thing one level in.
#}
{% macro comment(reply) %}
<li id="comment-{{ reply.id }}" class="comment h-entry comment-{{ reply.source }}">
  <article class="comment-body">
    <footer class="comment-meta">
      <div class="comment-author vcard p-author h-card">
        {%- if reply.author.avatar %}
        <img class="avatar u-photo" src="{{ reply.author.avatar }}" alt="" width="40" height="40" loading="lazy">
        {%- endif %}
        <b class="fn p-name">{% if reply.author.url %}<a class="url u-url" href="{{ reply.author.url }}" rel="nofollow ugc noopener noreferrer">{{ reply.author.name }}</a>{% else %}{{ reply.author.name }}{% endif %}</b>
      </div>
      <div class="comment-metadata">
        {%- if reply.url %}
        <a class="u-url" href="{{ reply.url }}"{% if reply.source != "comment" %} rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer"{% endif %}><time class="dt-published" datetime="{{ reply.published | date("iso") }}">{{ reply.published | date }}</time></a>
        {%- else %}
        <time class="dt-published" datetime="{{ reply.published | date("iso") }}">{{ reply.published | date }}</time>
        {%- endif %}
      </div>
    </footer>
    <div class="comment-content e-content">
      {{ reply.content | safe }}
    </div>
    {#
      Answering a comment on this site needs no JavaScript: the link carries
      the comment's id to the form, which puts it in a hidden field. A
      fediverse reply is answered on the server that holds it, and a webmention
      is answered on the page that sent it, so neither gets such a link.
    #}
    {% if commentForm and reply.source == "comment" %}
    <div class="reply"><a class="comment-reply-link" href="{{ page.url }}?reply_to={{ reply.id }}#respond">Reply</a></div>
    {% endif %}
  </article>
  {% if reply.replies.length %}
  <ol class="children">
    {% for child in reply.replies %}
    {{ comment(child) }}
    {% endfor %}
  </ol>
  {% endif %}
</li>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  The reactions, above the thread. A like and a boost come from the fediverse
  and a `repost` from a webmention; the CMS has already put a repost among the
  boosts, which is why there are three groups and not four. `p-like`,
  `p-repost` and `p-mention` are the source theme's classes for them.
#}
{% if conversation.likes.length or conversation.boosts.length or conversation.mentions.length %}
<div class="reactions-section">
  {{- group(conversation.likes, "Likes", "p-like", "❤️") }}
  {{- group(conversation.boosts, "Boosts", "p-repost", "🔁") }}
  {{- group(conversation.mentions, "Mentions", "p-mention", "🔗", true) }}
</div>
{% endif %}

{#
  And the thread. The title counts the whole of it rather than the top level,
  which is what `counts.replies` is, and names the post the way the source
  theme does.

  "Comments are closed." is printed under a post that has been answered and is
  no longer taking comments. That a post is closed is what the absence of
  `commentForm` says: the CMS puts the form on the context only while the site
  switch, the closing window and the post's own front matter all agree it is
  open, so there is nothing else for a theme to work out.
#}
{% if conversation.replies.length %}
<div id="comments" class="comments-area">
  <h2 class="comments-title">
    {%- if conversation.counts.replies == 1 %}One comment{% else %}{{ conversation.counts.replies }} comments{% endif %} on &ldquo;<span>{{ title }}</span>&rdquo;
  </h2>

  <ol class="comment-list">
    {% for reply in conversation.replies %}
    {{ comment(reply) }}
    {% endfor %}
  </ol>

  {% if not commentForm %}
  <p class="no-comments">Comments are closed.</p>
  {% endif %}
</div>
{% endif %}
